.SH NAME
ktm-guide-modules-and-packages \- Modules and packages (chapter 22 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A module is a directory of \fB\&.ktm\fR files with a name. A program imports it and uses the names the module marks \fBpub\fR, with the module's name in front: \fBgeo.area(5)\fR\&. A package is a module that lives in its own git repository and is imported by its path, as in Go. This chapter covers writing a module, what is visible where, methods and private fields across modules, imports and how modules are found, start-up order, the prelude, packages with \fBktm.mod\fR, \fBktm get\fR, and the prebuilt package cache.
.SH Importing a module
.PP
The standard library is a set of modules. \fBimport\fR makes one available by its name:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

i32 main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("modules");
    s.append(" and packages");
    printf("%.*s\en", s.len, s.ptr);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
modules and packages
.EE
.RE
.PP
Several modules are imported in one line, separated by commas:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import cstr, mem;

i32 main() {
    u8 *copy = cstr.dup("kept");
    printf("%s %d\en", copy, cstr.len(copy));
    mem.free(copy);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
kept 4
.EE
.RE
.PP
A module in a subdirectory is imported with dots, and used by its last name: \fBimport net.http;\fR loads the directory \fBnet/http\fR, whose files say \fBmodule http;\fR, and its names are \fBhttp.get\fR, \fBhttp.Request\fR\&.
.PP
\fBimport name = path;\fR gives the module another name in this file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import s = str;

i32 main() {
    scoped String greeting = s.from("aliased");
    printf("%.*s\en", greeting.len, greeting.ptr);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
aliased
.EE
.RE
.PP
An import that is never used is a warning, so leftover imports don't pile up.
.SH Writing a module
.PP
A module is a directory. Every file in it starts with \fBmodule\fR and the directory's name. Its \fBpub\fR declarations are what importers see; the rest is private to the module.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
shapes/
├── main.ktm
└── geo/
    ├── shapes.ktm
    └── helpers.ktm
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// geo/shapes.ktm
module geo;

pub struct Point { i64 x, y; };
pub enum Kind { square, circle };
pub const i64 SIZE = 4;

pub i64 area(Kind k, i64 side) {
    if (k == Kind.square) {
        return square(side);
    }
    return 3 * square(side);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// geo/helpers.ktm
module geo;

i64 square(i64 n) {
    return n * n;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// main.ktm
import geo;

i32 main() {
    geo.Point p = {1, 2};
    i64 cells[geo.SIZE];
    printf("%d %d %d\en", geo.area(geo.Kind.square, 5), p.y, sizeof(cells));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm main.ktm \-o shapes && ./shapes
25 2 32
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Inside the module, every name of every file is visible, in any order: no prototypes, and a struct can hold a struct of a later file.
.IP \(bu 2
Outside, only \fBpub\fR names are, with the module's name in front: \fBgeo.area\fR, \fBgeo.Point\fR, \fBgeo.Kind.square\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
A module's \fBconst\fR works in constant expressions outside it too: \fBi64 cells[geo.SIZE]\fR\&.
.PP
Using a private name from outside is an error:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat bad.ktm
import geo;

i32 main() {
    return geo.square(3);
}
$ ktm bad.ktm \-o bad
error: `square` is private to module geo
.EE
.RE
.SH Methods across modules
.PP
Only the module that defines a type can define its methods. Outside, its \fBpub\fR methods are called on a value with no prefix, and its private methods can't be called:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// bank/account.ktm
module bank;
import mem;

pub struct Account {
    u8 *owner;
    i64 balance;
};

pub Account *open(u8 *owner) {
    Account *a = mem.allocZero(sizeof(Account));
    a.owner = owner;
    return a;
}

pub void Account.deposit(i64 x) {
    self.balance += x;
    self.audit();
}

void Account.audit() {
    printf("audit: %d\en", self.balance);
}

pub void Account.close() {
    mem.free(self);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// main.ktm
import bank;

i32 main() {
    bank.Account *a = bank.open("ann");
    a.deposit(5);
    printf("%s %d\en", a.owner, a.balance);
    a.close();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-jit main.ktm
audit: 5
ann 5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Calling \fBa.audit()\fR from \fBmain.ktm\fR is an error: "the method \fBaudit\fR of \fBbank.Account\fR is private to module bank". \fB&bank.Account.deposit\fR is the method as a function pointer.
.SH Private fields
.PP
A field marked \fBprivate\fR can only be used inside the module that defines the struct:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// chat/conn.ktm
module chat;

pub struct Conn {
    i32 fd;
    private u8 *buf;
};

pub void reset(Conn *c) {
    c.buf = null;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// main.ktm
import chat;

i32 main() {
    chat.Conn c;
    c.fd = 3;
    chat.Conn copy = c;
    chat.reset(&c);
    printf("%d %d\en", copy.fd, sizeof(chat.Conn));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-jit main.ktm
3 16
.EE
.RE
.PP
Writing \fBc.buf = null;\fR in \fBmain.ktm\fR is an error: "\fBbuf\fR is a private field of chat.Conn".
.IP \(bu 2
Outside the module, reading, writing or taking the address of a private field is an error, and so is an initialiser list or a struct that inherits it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsizeof\fR, copying and passing by value still work: the layout isn't hidden, only the name.
.IP \(bu 2
Fields are public unless marked; \fBpub\fR on a field is an error. \fBprivate\fR fields exist only in modules.
.SH How modules are found
.PP
\fBimport geo;\fR looks for the directory \fBgeo\fR:
.IP 1. 4
next to the main file of the program;
.IP 2. 4
in the standard library;
.IP 3. 4
in each directory of the \fBKTM_PATH\fR environment variable, separated by \fB:\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ KTM_PATH=$HOME/ktm\-modules ktm main.ktm \-o app
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A module found in two places is an error: give your modules names the standard library doesn't use.
.IP \(bu 2
Imports come first in a file, before any declaration.
.IP \(bu 2
A cycle of imports is an error that shows it: "circular import: a -> b -> a".
.IP \(bu 2
A file whose \fBmodule\fR line is missing or doesn't match its directory is an error.
.SH Start-up order
.PP
Each module is compiled once, before the code that imports it. When the program starts, a module's globals are set up before its importers' globals and before \fBmain\fR, and a module's files in the order of their names.
.PP
A blank import, \fBimport _ = name;\fR, loads a module only for what it does when the program starts: \fBimport _ = net.https;\fR gives the HTTP client \fBhttps://\fR URLs. It is never reported as unused.
.SH The prelude
.PP
The prelude's names are seen by every file with no import and no prefix:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBError\fR, the struct a \fBcatch\fR binds (see Errors (see ktm-guide-errors(7)));
.IP \(bu 2
\fBEXIT_OK\fR and \fBEXIT_FAIL\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
the integer limits \fBI8_MIN\fR, \fBI8_MAX\fR, \fBU8_MAX\fR ... \fBI64_MIN\fR, \fBI64_MAX\fR, \fBU64_MAX\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERR_BOUNDS\fR, the code of an index out of range.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i32 main() {
    printf("%d %d %u\en", I32_MAX, I8_MIN, U16_MAX);
    printf("%d %d\en", EXIT_OK, EXIT_FAIL);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2147483647 \-128 65535
0 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
The runtime module \fBrt\fR is part of every program but internal: \fBimport rt;\fR outside the standard library is an error.
.SH A local with a module's name
.PP
A local variable or a parameter may have an import's name; within its scope the name is the local:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import str;

i64 count(u8[] str) {
    return str.len;
}

i32 main() {
    scoped String s = str.from("module again");
    printf("%d %.*s\en", count("four"), s.len, s.ptr);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4 module again
.EE
.RE
.PP
A global with an import's name is an error, since it would hide the module everywhere.
.SH Packages
.PP
A package is a module in its own git repository, imported by its path in quotes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import "github.com/ozzenerol/ktm\-pg";
.EE
.RE
.PP
Its name in the code is its \fBmodule\fR line, so this package is used as \fBpg.connect(...)\fR\&. \fBimport p = "github.com/user/repo";\fR gives it another name, and a directory of a package is a package too: \fBimport "github.com/user/repo/sub";\fR\&.
.SS ktm.mod
.PP
A project that uses packages has a \fBktm.mod\fR file at its root. \fBktm mod init\fR makes one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir hello && cd hello
$ ktm mod init example.com/me/hello
ktm: created ktm.mod for example.com/me/hello
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
module github.com/you/users\-api

ktm 0.23

require (
	github.com/ozzenerol/ktm\-pg v0.2.0
	github.com/someone/color v1.1.0 // indirect
)

replace github.com/someone/color => ../color
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmodule\fR is the project's own path; its other directories are imported by it too (\fBimport "github.com/you/users\-api/store";\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBktm\fR is the ktm version the project needs; an older compiler refuses it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrequire\fR lists each package the build uses and its version; \fB// indirect\fR marks one that only another package needs.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreplace\fR uses a local copy (a path starting with \fB\&./\fR, \fB\&../\fR or \fB/\fR) or another repository's version (\fB=> github.com/fork/color v1.1.2\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBktm.sum\fR, next to it, holds a hash of every version's files: a download that doesn't match is refused, so a version can't change under you. Commit both files.
.SS Getting, building and running
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm get github.com/ozzenerol/ktm\-pg
ktm: added github.com/ozzenerol/ktm\-pg v0.2.0
$ ktm build              # main.ktm of this directory, into ./users\-api
$ ktm run . \-\-port 8080  # or build and run it, with its arguments
.EE
.RE
.PP
A local package through \fBreplace\fR, end to end:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// ../greet/greet.ktm  (its ktm.mod says: module example.com/acme/greet)
module greet;

pub void hello(u8 *who) {
    printf("hello, %s\en", who);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
// main.ktm
import "example.com/acme/greet";

i32 main() {
    greet.hello("world");
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat ktm.mod
module example.com/me/hello

ktm 0.23

require example.com/acme/greet v0.0.0

replace example.com/acme/greet => ../greet
$ ktm build && ./hello
hello, world
$ ktm mod list
example.com/me/hello
example.com/acme/greet v0.0.0 => ../greet
.EE
.RE
.PP
